------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

A Verkada cellular gateway runs a handful of long-lived worker threads: the
modem manager, the cloud uploader, the PoE controller, a camera prober, a
config syncer. If one of them wedges — a driver ioctl that never returns, a
deadlock on the modem AT-command mutex — the box looks alive from the outside
but has quietly stopped doing its job. The SoC has a hardware watchdog to
recover from exactly this, and the platform team hands you its driver:

    void     hwwdt_kick(void);                    /* pet the dog             */
    void     hwwdt_report_fault(uint32_t id);     /* tell the platform who   */
    uint64_t hwwdt_now_us(void);                  /* shared us clock         */

  hwwdt_kick()          must be called at least once per HWWDT_TIMEOUT_US or
                        the SoC resets the board. One MMIO write, tens of ns,
                        callable from any thread — but the register is not a
                        synchronisation point, so YOU must make sure only one
                        thread ever writes it.
  hwwdt_report_fault()  cheap; ends up in a crash log / cloud event. The
                        harness records every call (time + worker id).
  HWWDT_TIMEOUT_US      2 s on the real part. THIS EXERCISE SCALES EVERYTHING
                        20x DOWN — hwwdt.h sets it to 100 ms and the harness
                        uses 4..500 ms worker deadlines — so a run takes ~4 s
                        instead of ~80 s. Nothing else about the problem changes.

Note how this is the MIRROR IMAGE of the usual "wrap the blocking vendor call"
problem. There is no blocking vendor call here. Instead there are MANY WRITERS
(every worker thread says "I am alive" on its hot path) and exactly ONE READER
(the thing that decides whether to pet the dog). All the cost must land on the
reader.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1. Concurrency — make checking in almost free.

      int  wd_init(void);
      void wd_deinit(void);
      int  wd_register(const char *name, uint64_t deadline_us);
      void wd_unregister(int id);
      void wd_heartbeat(uint32_t worker_id);      /* <-- the hot path */

  wd_heartbeat() is called by every worker inside its main loop, so it must be
  WAIT-FREE: one relaxed/release atomic store of a timestamp into that worker's
  own slot. No mutex, no CAS loop, no allocation, nothing that can block — and
  ideally no cache line shared with another worker (say the words "false
  sharing" and pad the slots).

  A monitor thread of your own wakes on a TIMED WAIT, works out who is overdue,
  calls hwwdt_report_fault() for each new death, and kicks the hardware
  watchdog ONLY IF EVERY REGISTERED WORKER IS HEALTHY. The last part is the
  whole point of the exercise: a thread that just kicks on a timer keeps the
  board alive precisely while the rest of the system is dead, which turns the
  hardware watchdog into a liveness lie. Each death must be reported exactly
  once, and reported again if the worker recovers and then dies a second time.

Part 2. Data structure — find who expires next without scanning everyone.

      int    wd_next_deadline(uint64_t *when_us);
      size_t wd_overdue(uint32_t *ids_out, size_t max);

  The naive monitor polls on a fixed period and scans all N workers per tick.
  Do better: keep the workers in a binary MIN-HEAP keyed by "when does this
  worker's deadline expire", so the monitor can sleep exactly until the next
  one, plus an index array (id -> heap position) so a worker's node is found in
  O(1) and unregister is O(log n) instead of O(n).

  The catch, and the thing to explain out loud: a heartbeat changes a key, but
  a heartbeat MUST NOT fix the heap — sifting is O(log n) and needs the lock,
  which puts a lock straight back on the hot path. So use LAZY UPDATES. Work
  out the invariant that makes them safe (hint: last_seen only moves forward,
  so a stored key is wrong in only one direction), and then work out why
  repairing only the ROOT is enough to answer "what is the earliest deadline?"
  exactly. State the complexity of register / unregister / heartbeat /
  next_deadline / overdue, and say when you would reach for a TIMER WHEEL
  instead (and what it costs you).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour (what the harness prints PASS/FAIL for)

  - Empty state: no worker registered -> wd_next_deadline() == -1,
    wd_overdue() == 0, and the dog IS still kicked (vacuously healthy).
  - Bad arguments: deadline 0 and a full table return -1; out-of-range ids in
    wd_heartbeat()/wd_unregister() are ignored, not a crash.
  - Cost: 8 threads x 40000 calls of wd_heartbeat() vs the same threads doing
    clock read + lock + store + unlock. The heartbeat must be at least 2.5x
    cheaper per call (the reference is ~10x: ~7 ns vs ~66 ns).
  - Part 2: wd_next_deadline() is compared with a brute-force minimum computed
    by the harness — after registration, after a heartbeat that hands the
    minimum to a different worker, and after the minimum holder unregisters.
  - Fault lifecycle: a worker that stops is reported EXACTLY ONCE, within
    [deadline, deadline + 60 ms] of its last heartbeat; a healthy neighbour is
    never reported; wd_overdue() names exactly the dead one; after it resumes
    wd_overdue() is empty again; if it dies a second time it is reported again.
  - Kick discipline: the fake hwwdt logs every kick with a timestamp. ZERO
    kicks are allowed while a worker is overdue, and the worst gap between
    kicks while everyone is healthy must stay inside HWWDT_TIMEOUT_US.
  - Stress: 8 workers beating every 4..9 ms for ~1.6 s with a 9th worker being
    registered and unregistered mid-flight; ~25 more next-deadline comparisons
    against brute force (the lazy-heap correctness test), no spurious faults.
    Also clean under -fsanitize=thread.
  - Shutdown: after wd_deinit() no further kick is recorded.
  Last line is exactly "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S) FAILED".

